1. `shim/profile.lua` — NEW FILE
Why it exists
Before this change `main.lua` hard-coded the assumption that a vBot profile lives at
`<otRoot>/profiles/bot/<config>`. The stock upstream layout is
`<otRoot>/mods/game_bot/default_configs/vBot_4.8`, which that parser rejected outright,
so a stock 4.8 tree could not be selected at all. This module is the single resolver for
both layouts and it never copies, downloads or creates anything.
What it supports for vBot
Selecting a stock `mods/game_bot/default_configs/vBot_4.8` directory.
Selecting a classic user `profiles/bot/<config>` directory (unchanged behaviour).
Deriving the otclient root, the `g_resources` write root, the `/bot/<config>` mount,
and the writable overlay list.
Environment variables `LUACLIENT_VBOT_PROFILE`, `LUACLIENT_VBOT_OTROOT`,
`LUACLIENT_VBOT_USERDIR`, with explicit CLI arguments taking precedence.
Full file content
```lua
-- Resolve an existing user profile or stock vBot configuration. No copying,
-- downloading, or directory creation: stock sources are mounted read-only.
local M = {}
local function normalise(path, trustedDefault)
if type(path) ~= 'string' or path == '' then return nil end
path = path:gsub('\', '/'):gsub('/+$', '')
if path:find('%z') then return nil end
for part in path:gmatch('[^/]+') do
if part == '..' and not trustedDefault then return nil end
end
return path
end
local function exists(path)
local f = io.open(path, 'rb')
if not f then return false end
f:close()
return true
end
-- Sub-paths of a stock configuration that hold user state rather than upstream code.
local USER_STATE = { 'storage', 'vBot_configs', 'cavebot_configs', 'targetbot_configs' }
-- opts.profileDir / opts.otRoot are explicit and take precedence over the
-- corresponding environment variables. opts.getenv supports isolated tests.
-- opts.userDir redirects writes for a stock configuration to the user's own tree.
function M.resolve(opts)
opts = opts or {}
local getenv = opts.getenv or os.getenv
local rawDir = opts.profileDir or getenv('LUACLIENT_VBOT_PROFILE')
local rawRoot = opts.otRoot or getenv('LUACLIENT_VBOT_OTROOT')
local rawUser = opts.userDir or getenv('LUACLIENT_VBOT_USERDIR')
local dir, otRoot = normalise(rawDir), normalise(rawRoot)
local userDirOpt = normalise(rawUser)
if rawDir and not dir then return nil, 'invalid vBot profile directory' end
if rawRoot and not otRoot then return nil, 'invalid otclient root directory' end
if rawUser and not userDirOpt then return nil, 'invalid vBot user directory' end
if not dir and otRoot then
local userDir = otRoot .. '/profiles/bot/vBot_4.8'
dir = exists(userDir .. '/_Loader.lua') and userDir
or otRoot .. '/mods/game_bot/default_configs/vBot_4.8'
end
-- The launcher's historical sibling-checkout candidates contain ../..;
-- preserve those trusted defaults without accepting traversal in CLI/env input.
dir = dir or normalise(opts.defaultProfile, true)
if not dir then
return nil, 'specify --vbot-profile=DIR or --vbot-otroot=DIR (an existing local checkout)'
end
local parent, config = dir:match('^(.)/([^/]+)$')
if not parent or config == '.' or config:find(':', 1, true) then
return nil, 'invalid vBot profile directory: ' .. dir
end
local stockRoot = parent:match('^(.)/mods/game_bot/default_configs$')
local writeDir = parent:match('^(.)/[Bb]ot$')
if stockRoot then
otRoot = otRoot or stockRoot
writeDir = otRoot .. '/profiles'
elseif writeDir then
otRoot = otRoot or writeDir:match('^(.)/[^/]+$')
else
return nil, 'vBot profile must be under bot/ or mods/game_bot/default_configs/'
end
if not otRoot or otRoot == '' then
return nil, 'cannot derive the otclient root; pass --vbot-otroot=DIR'
end
local overlays
if stockRoot and userDirOpt then
writeDir = userDirOpt
overlays = {}
for _, name in ipairs(USER_STATE) do
overlays[#overlays + 1] = '/bot/' .. config .. '/' .. name
end
end
return {
dir = dir, config = config, otRoot = otRoot, writeDir = writeDir,
stock = stockRoot ~= nil,
-- Mount only the selected configuration, not the rest of the checkout.
mounts = stockRoot and { ['/bot/' .. config] = dir } or nil,
-- Stock code stays read-only; only these state directories are writable.
overlays = overlays,
}
end
return M
```
Line-by-line rationale
Lines
What it does
Why
`normalise(path, trustedDefault)`
Converts `` to `/`, strips trailing slashes, rejects NUL, rejects any `..` segment
CLI and environment input must not escape the intended tree. `trustedDefault` exists because the launcher's own historical fallback candidates legitimately contain `../..`; only those bypass the `..` rule
`exists(path)`
Plain `io.open` probe
Used to prefer an existing user profile over the stock config when only `--vbot-otroot` is given. Deliberately does not create anything
`USER_STATE`
`storage`, `vBot_configs`, `cavebot_configs`, `targetbot_configs`
These four directories are the only places stock vBot 4.8 keeps user state: bot storage (macro on/off + settings), HealBot/AttackBot JSON, CaveBot `.cfg` routes, TargetBot JSON. Everything else in the tree is upstream code and must stay frozen
`rawDir/rawRoot/rawUser`
Explicit option first, environment second
Explicit CLI beats environment; documented behaviour
`if not dir and otRoot`
With only a root, prefer `profiles/bot/vBot_4.8` if it has `_Loader.lua`, else stock `default_configs/vBot_4.8`
A user who has customised their profile keeps using it; a fresh checkout falls back to the shipped 4.8 config
`parent, config = dir:match(...)`
Splits the final path component as the config name
`config` becomes the `/bot/<config>` virtual directory that upstream `_Loader.lua` and `executor.lua` build paths from
`config == '.'` / `config:find(':')` guard
Rejects degenerate names
`/bot/.` or a drive-letter fragment would produce a nonsense mount
`stockRoot = parent:match('^(.)/mods/game_bot/default_configs$')`
Detects the stock layout
This is the exact upstream directory shape; matching it is what makes stock support possible
`writeDir = parent:match('^(.)/[Bb]ot$')`
Detects the classic layout
`Bb` because Windows checkouts sometimes capitalise `Bot`
stock branch sets `writeDir = otRoot .. '/profiles'`
Keeps `g_resources` rooted where the real client roots it
vBot resolves everything relative to the profiles root
final `error` when neither matched
Loud refusal
Better than silently running with a wrong root
overlay block
When stock and `userDir` given, write root becomes `userDir` and the four state paths become overlays
This is what makes a stock profile usable in practice: it can persist storage and configs without the stock tree ever being written
`mounts = stockRoot and { ['/bot/'..config] = dir }`
Mounts only the chosen config
Not the whole checkout — the mount is deliberately narrow
2. `shim/resources.lua` — read-only mounts and writable overlays
Why
`g_resources` previously mapped every virtual path to `<writeDir><virtualPath>` and
nothing else. To run a stock vBot tree the loader must read `/bot/vBot_4.8/...` from
the checkout's `mods/game_bot/default_configs/vBot_4.8` directory while that directory
stays byte-identical. Mounts provide the read path; overlays provide a writable
sub-path so user state can still be saved.
What it supports for vBot
`_Loader.lua` line 4: `g_resources.listDirectoryFiles("/bot/"..configName.."/vBot", true, false)`
works against stock sources, including the recursive `.otui` style discovery.
`executor.lua`'s double-slash paths (`/bot/vBot_4.8//vBot/main.lua`) still resolve.
`vBotConfigSave(...)`, `CaveBot.save()`, `TargetBot.save()` and bot storage writes
land in the user's own directory instead of being refused outright.
Diff
```diff
@@ SANDBOX doc block
Every virtual path resolves to <writeDir> .. <virtualPath> and nothing else.
Virtual paths resolve beneath writeDir, except explicitly supplied read-only
directory mounts (used for stock vBot configurations). Mounts shadow the same
virtual subtree in writeDir; writes and deletion of mount ancestors are refused.
```
```diff
@@ function RES:resolvePath(path)
return collapse(full)
full = collapse(full)
-- Canonicalise harmless dot segments before matching mount boundaries.
local parts = {}
for part in full:gmatch('[^/]+') do
if part ~= '.' then parts[#parts + 1] = part end
end
return '/' .. table.concat(parts, '/')
```
Why: mount matching is a string-prefix test. Without dropping `.` segments,
`/bot/./vBot_4.8/vBot/main.lua` would miss the `/bot/vBot_4.8` mount and fall through to
the write tree, producing a "file not found" for a file that plainly exists. `..` is
still refused later in `_hostPath`; only the harmless `.` is canonicalised.
```diff
+local function beneath(path, root)
return root == '/' or path == root or path:sub(1, #root + 1) == root .. '/'
+end

+--- The overlay a virtual path belongs to, or nil. An overlay is a sub-path of a
+--- read-only mount that the user's own writable tree owns instead.
+function RES:_overlay(v)
for _, overlay in ipairs(self._overlays) do
if beneath(v, overlay) then return overlay end
end
return nil
+end

function RES:_real(path, mode)
local v = self:resolvePath(path)
local host_, why = self:_hostPath(v)
if host_ then return host_, v end
if host_ then
local overlaid = self:_overlay(v) ~= nil
for _, mount in ipairs(self._mounts) do
if beneath(v, mount.path) then
-- Inside an overlay the user's copy wins, and stock stays the fallback.
if overlaid then
if mode == 'write' or host.isFile(host_) or host.isDir(host_) then
return host_, v
end
return mount.dir .. v:sub(#mount.path + 1), v
end
if mode == 'write' then
self._refusals[v] = 'read-only mount ' .. mount.path
return nil, v
end
return mount.dir .. v:sub(#mount.path + 1), v
end
if mode == 'write' and beneath(mount.path, v) then
self._refusals[v] = 'read-only mount ' .. mount.path
return nil, v
end
end
return host_, v
end
```
Key semantics, in order of the checks:
Inside an overlay — writes always go to the user tree; reads prefer the user's
copy and fall back to the stock file when the user has not saved one yet. This is
what lets a stock `HealBot.json` be read on first run and a modified one be read

Inside a mount, not overlaid — writes are refused and recorded in `_refusals`;
reads are redirected to the mounted host directory.
An ancestor of a mount (e.g. `/bot` when the mount is `/bot/vBot_4.8`) — writes
and deletes refused, so the mount cannot be removed from underneath.
Mounts are sorted longest-path-first at construction, so the most specific mount wins.
```diff
function RES:fileExists(path)
local real = self:_real(path, 'predicate')
local real, v = self:_real(path, 'predicate')
if not real then return false end
for _, mount in ipairs(self._mounts) do
if beneath(mount.path, v) and host.isDir(mount.dir) then return false end
end
return host.isFile(real)
end
function RES:directoryExists(path)
local real = self:_real(path, 'predicate')
local real, v = self:_real(path, 'predicate')
if not real then return false end
for _, mount in ipairs(self._mounts) do
if beneath(mount.path, v) and host.isDir(mount.dir) then return true end
end
return host.isDir(real)
end
```
Why: the virtual parents of a mount must behave like directories even when they do not
exist on disk. With a mount at `/bot/vBot_4.8` and no real `profiles/bot` directory,
`directoryExists('/bot')` must be true and `fileExists('/bot')` must be false —
`bot.lua` discriminates on exactly that pair.
```diff
local names = host.list(real)
if not raw then
-- An overlaid directory exists in both trees; the union is what the bot sees.
if self:_overlay(path) then
... merge the entries of the counterpart directory ...
end
-- A mount also exposes its virtual parent directories, even when the
-- user has never created a profiles/bot directory on disk.
local seen = {}
for _, name in ipairs(names) do seen[name] = true end
for _, mount in ipairs(self._mounts) do
if mount.path ~= path and beneath(mount.path, path) and host.isDir(mount.dir) then
local suffix = mount.path:sub(path == '/' and 2 or #path + 2)
local name = suffix:match('^[^/]+')
if name and not seen[name] then
names[#names + 1] = name
seen[name] = true
end
end
end
end
```
Why the union matters for vBot: CaveBot and TargetBot list their config directories to
build the profile dropdowns. Without merging, a user who saves one route into the
overlay would see only that route and lose every shipped one. The existing sort at the
end of the function is untouched, so ordering stays byte-wise as upstream expects.
```diff
function RES:readFileContents(path)
local real = self:_real(path, 'read')
local real, v = self:_real(path, 'read')
for _, mount in ipairs(self._mounts) do
if beneath(mount.path, v) and host.isDir(mount.dir) then
error(("unable to read file '%s': is a mounted directory"):format(v), 2)
end
end
```
Why: reading a virtual mount parent as a file must raise, matching the C++ behaviour
that `bot.lua` and `vBot/configs.lua` wrap in `pcall`.
```diff
function RES:makeDir(path)
local real = self:_real(path, 'write')
if not real then return false end
function RES:writeFileContents(path, data)
local real = self:_real(path, 'write')
if not real then return false end
function RES:deleteFile(path)
local real = self:_real(path, 'write')
if not real then return false end
```
Why: `_real` now returns `nil` for a refused write. Without these guards the refusal
would be a crash inside `io.open(nil)` instead of the `false` the real `g_resources`
returns on failure — and vBot calls these without `pcall`.
```diff
local impl = setmetatable({
...
_mounts = {},
_overlays = {},
}, RES)
for virtual, dir in pairs(opts.mounts or {}) do
-- validate shape, resolve, reject '/' and duplicates
impl._mounts[#impl._mounts + 1] = { path = path, dir = dir:gsub('\','/'):gsub('/+$','') }
end
table.sort(impl._mounts, function(a, b) return #a.path > #b.path end)

for _, virtual in ipairs(opts.overlays or {}) do
-- An overlay equal to (or above) a mount would unlock the whole stock tree.
if beneath(path, mount.path) and path ~= mount.path then inside = true end
if not inside then
error('resources.new: overlay must be inside a mount: ' .. virtual, 2)
end
impl._overlays[#impl._overlays + 1] = path
end
```
The overlay validation is the security-relevant part: an overlay of `/bot/vBot_4.8` or
`/bot` would make the entire stock tree writable, defeating the whole point, so both are
rejected at construction time rather than at first write.
Not changed: the sandbox remains lexical. A symlink planted inside the profile can
still point outside it, exactly as documented before and exactly as PhysFS behaves.
3. `shim/host.lua` — write allow-list
Why
The host's read-only guard was all-or-nothing: either every `g_resources` write was
refused, or `--vbot-write` unlocked everything. With stock sources that meant bot storage
could never be persisted at all, so macro on/off state and every configuration edit was
lost on exit.
Diff
```diff
h.readOnly = opts.readOnly ~= false
-- Virtual prefixes that stay writable in readOnly mode (the user's own overlay).
h.writeAllow = opts.writeAllow or {}
```
```diff
local log = self.log
local allow = self.writeAllow or {}
local function permitted(path)
if type(path) ~= 'string' then return false end
local ok, v = pcall(res.resolvePath, path)
if not ok or type(v) ~= 'string' then return false end
for _, prefix in ipairs(allow) do
if v == prefix or v:sub(1, #prefix + 1) == prefix .. '/' then return true end
end
return false
end
local function refuse(what)
local real = res[what]
return function(path, ...)
if permitted(path) then return real(path, ...) end
blocked[#blocked + 1] = what .. ' ' .. tostring(path)
```
Details that matter:
`res.resolvePath` is applied before matching, so `/bot/vBot_4.8//storage/x.json`
and `/bot/./vBot_4.8/storage/x.json` are matched the same way the resources layer will
ultimately resolve them. Matching raw argument strings would be bypassable.
The prefix test requires either an exact match or a following `/`. A sibling path such
as `/bot/vBot_4.8/storage-elsewhere.json` therefore does not match

`local real = res[what]` is captured when the guard is installed, i.e. before the
wrapper replaces the field, so an allowed write reaches the genuine implementation.
Every refusal is still appended to `blockedWrites`; nothing became silent.
```diff
function H:saveStorage()
if not self.context then return false, 'not started' end
if self.readOnly then
self.blockedWrites[...] = 'saveStorage ' .. self:_storagePath()
local path = self:_storagePath()
if self.readOnly and not self:_writeAllowed(path) then
self.blockedWrites[...] = 'saveStorage ' .. path
return false, 'read-only'
end

return res.writeFileContents(self:_storagePath(), txt) and true or false
return res.writeFileContents(path, txt) and true or false
+end

+--- True when `path` is inside a configured writable overlay prefix.
+function H:_writeAllowed(path)
for _, prefix in ipairs(self.writeAllow or {}) do
if path == prefix or path:sub(1, #prefix + 1) == prefix .. '/' then return true end
end
return false
end
```
What this supports for vBot: `/bot/<config>/storage/profile_<N>.json` carries
`_macros` — the user's own macro on/off switches. Persisting it is what makes a CLI
session resumable instead of resetting every macro on each start.
4. `shim/g_game.lua` — server walk responses
Why (this is the one real gameplay-correctness fix)
The shim maintained an optimistic pre-walk queue and only cleared it on a timeout
(step duration + ping, capped at 1000 ms). The two server packets that answer a walk were
parsed but never applied to the player model:
`0xB5 CancelWalk` — the server refused/undid the step.
`0xB6 WalkWait` — the server imposes a pause.
Consequences before the fix: after a refused step, `player:getPosition()` kept reporting
a tile the character never reached for up to a second, and every CaveBot/TargetBot
decision taken in that window used the wrong position. During a walk-wait the bot kept
sending walk packets that could not succeed.
Diff
```diff
-- Game::processWalkCancel -> LocalPlayer::cancelWalk: the server REFUSED the step, so
-- the optimistic tile has to go now. Waiting for the prewalk timeout instead left
-- getPosition() reporting a tile the character never reached for up to a second, and
-- every bot decision in that window used it.
local cancelHandle, waitHandle
if LC.events and LC.events.on then
cancelHandle = busOn(LC.events, 'walkCancel', function() clearPreWalks() end)
-- Game::processWalkWait -> LocalPlayer::lockWalk(millis): a server-imposed pause.
waitHandle = busOn(LC.events, 'walkWait', function(d)
local ms = d and tonumber(d.millis)
if not ms or ms <= 0 then return end
local player = reg:localPlayer()
if player and player.lockWalk then pcall(player.lockWalk, player, ms) end
end)
end
```
```diff
function g._shutdown()
if posHandle and LC.events and LC.events.off then busOff(LC.events, posHandle) end
posHandle = nil
if LC.events and LC.events.off then
for _, h in pairs({ posHandle, cancelHandle, waitHandle }) do busOff(LC.events, h) end
end
posHandle, cancelHandle, waitHandle = nil, nil, nil
```
Notes:
`busOn` already supports both the module form (`bus.on`) and the instance form
(`bus:on`) used by the hub worker and tests; the new handlers reuse it unchanged.
`pairs({...})` on the handle list skips `nil` entries, so shutdown is safe when the
event bus was absent at construction.
`lockWalk`/`isWalkLocked`/`canWalk` already existed in `shim/creature.lua`; nothing
called `lockWalk` before, so this wires up existing but dead machinery.
The parser already emitted both events (`proto/parser.lua`, `S[0xB5]`, `S[0xB6]`);
the native bot layer (`bot/walker.lua`) already consumed `walkCancel`. Only the shim
path — the one that runs real vBot — was missing it.
5. `shim/callbacks.lua` — census separation
Why
`fire()` (executor callback dispatch) and `gsignal()` (the `connect(g_game, {...})`
fan-out) both incremented the same `fired`/`dropped` counters. Because vBot connects to
`g_game` for some of the very same event names, one parser event was counted twice, so
the diagnostic census could not answer "did the executor callback run?" at all.
Diff
```diff
+--- `fired`/`dropped` count EXECUTOR dispatches; `signalled`/`signalDropped` count the
+--- separate `connect(g_game, ...)` fan-out, which shares event names but not channels.
function Handle:stats()
return { fired = self.fired, dropped = self.dropped, wired = self.wired }
return { fired = self.fired, dropped = self.dropped, wired = self.wired,
signalled = self.signalled, signalDropped = self.signalDropped }
end
```
```diff
fired = {}, dropped = {}, wired = {},
signalled = {}, signalDropped = {},
```
```diff
local function gsignal(name, ...)
local slot = gg and rawget(gg, name)
if slot == nil then
H.dropped[name] = (H.dropped[name] or 0) + 1
H.signalDropped[name] = (H.signalDropped[name] or 0) + 1
return
end
H.fired[name] = (H.fired[name] or 0) + 1
H.signalled[name] = (H.signalled[name] or 0) + 1
```
Behaviour is otherwise identical: both channels still dispatch, still `pcall`, still log
a raising callback. Only the accounting changed. This is diagnostics, not gameplay, but
it is what makes "is this vBot callback actually firing?" answerable when debugging a CLI
session.
6. `shim/patches.lua` — optional CLI adaptation
Why
Stock `vBot/main.lua` runs an update check at load:
```lua
if os.time() > storage.checkVersion + (12 * 60 * 60) then
storage.checkVersion = os.time()
HTTP.get("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Vithrax/vBot/main/vBot/version.txt", ...)
end
```
The shim deliberately installs no HTTP API (`shim/bootstrap.lua` sets `G.HTTP = nil`),
because HTTP is out of scope for CLI automation. With empty storage (`checkVersion = 0`)
that branch is taken on the first run and the missing global aborts profile loading.
The existing patch mechanism could not express this: every entry was mandatory and raised
when its text was absent, which would break any custom profile that does not contain the
updater.
Diff
```diff
+-- Optional CLI-only adaptations, separate from the mandatory object-model
+-- fixes above. A profile may omit these features entirely; absence is not drift.
+-- Only the exact known block is adapted, never arbitrary HTTP calls or scripts.
+-- Reference: Vithrax/vBot@09d6816c817f881735ecf78e87e052fcf7c02816.
+patches.OPTIONAL = {
['/vBot/main.lua'] = {
{
expected = 'if os.time() > storage.checkVersion + (12 * 60 * 60) then',
replacement = 'if false then -- CLI: optional vBot update check disabled',
why = 'CLI automation excludes the optional HTTP version check; no HTTP API is installed',
},
},
+}
```
```diff
function patches.has(virtualPath)
return patches.LIST[virtualPath] ~= nil
return patches.LIST[virtualPath] ~= nil or patches.OPTIONAL[virtualPath] ~= nil
end
```
```diff
function patches.apply(src, virtualPath, notes)
local list = patches.LIST[virtualPath]
local list = patches.LIST[virtualPath] or patches.OPTIONAL[virtualPath]
local optional = patches.LIST[virtualPath] == nil
if not list or type(src) ~= 'string' then return src end
for _, p in ipairs(list) do
local from, to = src:find(p.expected, 1, true)
if not from then
if not from and not optional then
upstream drifted ...)
end
if src:find(p.expected, to + 1, true) then
error(... matches more than once ...)
if from then
if src:find(p.expected, to + 1, true) then
error(... matches more than once ...)
end
src = src:sub(1, from - 1) .. p.replacement .. src:sub(to + 1)
if notes then notes[#notes + 1] = virtualPath .. ': ' .. p.why end
end
src = ...
end
return src
end
```
Deliberate properties:
Mandatory patches are unchanged: a missing `expected` in `functions/map.lua`,
`functions/ui_elements.lua` or `/vBot/training.lua` still raises loudly. Drift
detection is intact.
Optional patches tolerate absence but still raise on a duplicate match, because
rewriting an ambiguous signature is worse than not rewriting at all.
The replacement is `if false then`, which keeps the block syntactically valid and the
`end` balanced. Nothing else in `main.lua` is touched, so the UI labels and the rest of
the script load normally.
This is not a general HTTP suppressor: an unrelated `HTTP.get` line elsewhere is
left exactly as written and will still fail loudly.
Upstream files on disk are never modified (invariant I9); the rewrite is in memory on
the way to `load`.
Also fixed in this file: the indentation of the new block and of `patches.apply` was
normalised to the file's 4-space style.
7. `shim/bootstrap.lua` — threading the new options
```diff
--- opts.writeDir the g_resources root default <otRoot>/profiles
+--- opts.mounts read-only virtual directory -> host directory mappings
+--- opts.overlays writable sub-paths of a mount (user storage/configs)
```
```diff
G.g_resources = resources.new(writeDir)
G.g_resources = resources.new(writeDir, { mounts = opts.mounts, overlays = opts.overlays })
```
```diff
readOnly = opts.readOnly ~= false,
writeAllow = opts.overlays,
```
The overlay list is the single source of truth: the same array both configures the
resources layer and defines the host guard's allow-list, so the two cannot disagree.
8. `main.lua` — CLI surface
8.1 Help text
```diff
--vbot-profile=DIR the /bot/<config> directory to run, e.g.
.../otclient/profiles/bot/vBot_4.8. Implies --vbot.
--vbot-profile=DIR an existing profiles/bot/<config> or
mods/game_bot/default_configs/vBot_4.8 directory. Implies
--vbot. Stock configurations are always read-only.
--vbot-otroot=DIR override the otclient checkout (READ-ONLY) the shim reads
mods/game_bot and modules/ from
mods/game_bot and modules/ from. With no profile,
selects profiles/bot/vBot_4.8, then the stock config.
Environment: LUACLIENT_VBOT_PROFILE / LUACLIENT_VBOT_OTROOT.
--vbot-userdir=DIR keep a stock configuration read-only but persist storage,
vBot_configs, cavebot_configs and targetbot_configs under
DIR. Requires --vbot-write to actually save.
Environment: LUACLIENT_VBOT_USERDIR.
```
8.2 Argument parsing
```diff
or name == 'vbot-profile' or name == 'vbot-otroot'
or name == 'vbot-profile' or name == 'vbot-otroot' or name == 'vbot-userdir'
```
```diff
elseif name == 'vbot-userdir' then
if type(v) ~= 'string' or v == '' then
return nil, '--vbot-userdir needs a directory'
end
for part in tostring(v):gmatch('[^/\]+') do
if part == '..' then
return nil, '--vbot-userdir must not contain a ".." path component'
end
end
cfg.vbotUserDir = v
cfg.vbot = true
```
The `..` rejection mirrors the existing rule for `--bot-profile` and `--vbot-profile`;
the value becomes a write root, so traversal would redirect writes outside the tree.
8.3 Path resolution replaced by the shared resolver
```diff
-local function resolveVBotPaths(cfg)
local dir = cfg.vbotProfile or defaultBotProfile()
... 20 lines that only understood <profiles>/bot/<config> ...
-end
+local function resolveVBotPaths(cfg)
return require('shim.profile').resolve({
profileDir = cfg.vbotProfile, otRoot = cfg.vbotOtRoot,
userDir = cfg.vbotUserDir,
defaultProfile = defaultBotProfile(),
})
+end
```
This deletion is the change that actually unblocks stock vBot 4.8: the old parser
returned an error for any directory not under `bot/`.
8.4 Startup
```diff
if paths.stock and cfg.vbotWrite and not paths.overlays then
log.warn('vbot: stock configuration is read-only; --vbot-write cannot modify it '
.. '(use --vbot-userdir=DIR to keep your own storage and configs)')
end
```
```diff
local readOnly = (not cfg.vbotWrite) or (cfg.dryRun and true or false)
local readOnly = paths.stock or (not cfg.vbotWrite) or (cfg.dryRun and true or false)
-- Stock sources stay immutable either way; the overlay only unlocks user state.
local overlays = (cfg.vbotWrite and not cfg.dryRun) and paths.overlays or nil
```
```diff
otRoot = paths.otRoot,
writeDir = paths.writeDir,
mounts = paths.mounts,
overlays = overlays,
```
Resulting policy:
Invocation
Stock files
User state
stock profile, no `--vbot-write`
read-only
not saved
stock profile, `--vbot-write`, no `--vbot-userdir`
read-only (warned)
not saved
stock profile, `--vbot-write` + `--vbot-userdir=DIR`
read-only
saved under `DIR`
classic user profile, `--vbot-write`
writable as before
saved as before
any profile with `--dry-run`
read-only
not saved
Reproducing on another machine
Copy the eight production files listed in the summary table (or apply the diffs above).
Nothing else is required: no new dependencies, no generated assets, no downloads.
Provide an existing otclient checkout yourself. The runtime still needs
`mods/game_bot/executor.lua`, `functions/`, `panels/` and `modules/` — a vBot
configuration directory alone is not a runtime.
Typical CLI invocations:
```bash
stock config, read-only
luajit main.lua --vbot-otroot=/path/to/otclient
stock config with persistent user state
luajit main.lua --vbot-profile=/path/to/otclient/mods/game_bot/default_configs/vBot_4.8 \
--vbo